{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202611192334196325f83d00264ef7","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"B2 done: fixture custody test green on JVM (6 assertions). The fixture on disk is the true 5,403-byte artifact (sha 9673c614…).\n\nNow B3 — the LCG repair. Current code:\n\n```clojure\n(defn- lcg\n  \"The recorded-seed Lehmer generator x' = 48271*x mod (2^31-2). Every\n   product stays below 2^53, so every host computes the identical sequence\n   in its double arithmetic (a long-math generator would not be portable).\"\n  [seed n]\n  (loop [out [] x seed i 0]\n    (if (= i n)\n      out\n      (let [x' (mod (* 48271 x) 2147483646)]\n        (recur (conj out x') x' (inc i))))))\n```\n\nThe flaw: seed 1795017757000 > 2^53/48271, so the FIRST product 48271*seed exceeds 2^53. Fix: normalize the seed on entry: `(mod seed 2147483646)` — then every subsequent product ≤ 48271 * 2147483645 ≈ 1.036e14 < 2^53 ✓. Verify the architect's example: seed 1795017757000 mod 2147483646 = ? Then first state = 48271 * that mod m — same on JVM/Node/Dart ✓.\n\nThen \"pin/check the generated vectors across hosts\": add recorded literal expectations of `recorded-permutations` outputs for both seeds, asserted in a test — every host's generator provably produces the pinned vectors.\n\nThen the cross-host corpus reference. Design:\n\n```clojure\n(def ^:private corpus-reference-snapshot-cases ...) ;; recorded digests per case\n(deftest corpus-cross-host-reference-test ...)\n```\n\nWhat to record: per the architect \"Record one independently reviewed reference for the broader corpus's canonical bytes/content roots, full C1/key vectors and per-format provenance; compare every host to that same reference.\" — the digest-snapshot (root/applications/keys-digest/c1-digest/provenance-digest) per corpus case IS that bundle (same model as the cc4 cohort, which the architect accepted as \"correctly pinned\"). Cases: the 7 map-corpus cases + 4 set-corpus cases + map sizes 8/9/16/32 + set sizes 8/9/16/32 = 19 cases. Each built in the FORWARD (recorded) order via build-map/build-set (array-map rep... use the insertion-map? build-map :array-map rep — on cljd it's hash-map; the SNAPSHOT must be identical cross-host — post-fix it is, since the compiled tree is canonical regardless of representation). Use ONE canonical construction per case: `(build-map :array-map entries)` (forward order) for maps; `(build-set :vec-set elements)` for sets.\n\nRecord the digests via a capture namespace on the JVM, paste as literals `corpus-reference` (a vector of {:case … :root … :applications … :keys-digest … :c1-digest … :instruction-provenance-digest}). New deftest asserts equality with computed snapshots. Also assert `applications` counts.\n\nWait — one wrinkle: `digest-snapshot`'s `keys` includes origin `[o r path]` triples from project-batch with token \"order-a\" — deterministic ✓.\n\nLet me also double-check the corpus cases compile deterministically cross-host — the r2 within-host equality held on all three hosts, and the recorded reference pins it.\n\nWhich sizes get recorded? The architect named \"heterogeneous, nested, NaN, and size-threshold corpus cases\". NaN — hmm, the map corpus no longer has a NaN case (removed in r1); the set corpus has :colliding-value-expressions (which has... no NaN). The corpus is: constant-keys, dynamic-keys, heterogeneous, nested, floats, duplicate-subtrees + (same-key-different-values was removed). Plus set: dynamic-elements, nested-dynamic-sets, colliding-value-expressions, heterogeneous-elements. Plus sizes. That's the \"broader corpus\". Record ALL of them.\n\nB4 next. Let me now write the B3 code changes.","signature":"eae59d933d82444ea3b6dca9"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"e172e731-f0dd-446e-9ff9-20299ec6ae47"