n — must derive which pair sorts first); expected log = the first pair's taps IF the first pair sorts before the failing pair, else []. Let me construct: pairs A = [(tap :early)(tap :early-v)] and B = [(boom)] with value :never. Canonical order: whichever of [A-key-root A-val-root] vs [boom-root :never-root] is smaller comes first. Derive expected prefix = A's taps when A < B else []. Assert EXACTLY that on EVERY route (not some-of).\n- Every route raises: assert `:raised` on ALL FOUR routes (walker/semantic/stack/register all surfaced the ex-info in r2 — trace-of catches per lane; the r2 assertion was `(some :raised ...)`; strengthen to every?). Do all four routes raise? In r2 the failure test passed with some — need to check each lane's actual behavior. Empirically verify; if a route legitimately doesn't raise (e.g., returns an error value instead), record exactly what it does and assert THAT exactly.\n- Continuation transfer fixture: research how continuations work in this VM. Look at yin.vm.continuation-invoke-test and how a guest program captures/transfers. If there's a `call/cc`-like primitive or `vm/current-continuation` node usable from yang.clojure... The yang frontend has `:vm/current-continuation` node type. Is there reader syntax? Probably not exposed in the clojure frontend. The continuation family (clj/try etc.) is the outcome lane. Hmm — \"continuation-transfer\" per RULING 2: \"An exception or transfer of control during an operand prevents the later evaluations\" — the ruling's test 5: \"no later event after an operand failure/continuation transfer\". What fixtures exist for continuation transfer in the codebase? Search for tests exercising current-continuation with effects. Let me look at continuation-invoke-test.\n\n**B4.3 — set effect-order trace**: new block: dynamic set with taps incl. two colliding-value expressions:\n```clojure\n#{(tap :e1) (tap :e2) (tap :e3)} where e1/e2 collide at runtime\n```\nAssert per route: log = canonical element order (derived independently), each occurrence exactly once (log contains each tap arg once), final value has collided value once.\n\nLet me research the continuation support first.","signature":"a1d00db6ff3044e1aa4e5f07"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"957e19bb-f551-4696-8466-c3444c7c8f3b","timestamp":"2026-11-19T15:40:55.119Z","thinking_duration_ms":14815,"thinking_display":"updates"}
