{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261119234108fc5453ce251541ac","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The continuation machinery: `:vm/current-continuation` nodes are built via RAW AST helpers (not through yang.clojure's compiler — the clojure frontend has no `(current-continuation)` form). But RULING 2's continuation-transfer requirement is about the EMITTER's operand vectors: \"An exception or transfer of control during an operand prevents the later evaluations that the existing VM would not reach\" — the test need: a CONSTRUCTED COLLECTION whose operand invokes a captured continuation mid-vector, escaping before later operands evaluate.\n\nHow to build such a program? Compose the raw-AST continuation capture with a yang-compiled collection? The test namespace can build a HYBRID tree: compile the map via yang/compile, then splice a continuation-invoke into an operand? That changes the compiled tree (hand-surgery) — but for a SEMANTICS test (does the VM stop consuming operands after a transfer), a hand-built tree in the emitter's OUTPUT SHAPE (assoc over seed with tap/escape operands) is exactly the right fixture: the emitter's product shape, with an operand that captures and escapes.\n\nSimpler design mirroring escape-discards-pending-frames:\n```\n(+ 1 (with-k (if (= r 7) r (+ 1000 (r 7)))))\n```\nAdapted to a constructed-map operand vector: the collection `(assoc {} (tap :before) (tap :before-v) (escape) :after)` where `(escape)` is a subtree: captures the continuation and immediately invokes it with a value — the invoke ABORTS the pending assoc application (discards the constructor frame) → later operands (tap :after) never evaluate → program value = the continuation's delivered value at the capture point.\n\nUsing raw AST nodes for the escape operand, spliced into a yang-compiled constructor? OR build the whole program in raw AST in the emitter's shape. The cleanest semantics fixture: raw AST (like continuation-invoke-test) where the \"collection\" is an application node with operand vector [seed tap-operand... escape-operand tap-after...] — the EXACT shape the emitter emits — asserting: taps before the escape appear once; tap :after NEVER; final value is the escape's delivered value.\n\nBut hmm — is that testing MY emitter? The emitter's job is ordering; the ruling's semantics claim is about what the VM does with the ordered vector. A raw fixture in the emitted shape validates the claim \"an operand transfer prevents later evaluations\" for constructor-shaped applications. Combined with the emitter's ordering (tested elsewhere), the composition holds. Also alternative: compose via yang.compile with a defmacro? No — the clojure frontend can't express capture.\n\nAlso — could I splice: compile `{(tap :k) (tap :v) (esc) :after}` via yang with `esc` a FREE SYMBOL bound in env to... a reified continuation? Env values that are continuation objects: `values/continuation?` — the engine treats a continuation value in operator position... `(esc)` as an application of a variable bound to a captured continuation — invoking it transfers! Can env supply a continuation? The continuation VALUE comes from a capture INSIDE the program — chicken-and-egg for a one-expression program, but: the program itself could first capture (via raw subtree?) — no yang syntax.\n\nDECISION: build the transfer fixture as a raw-AST program in the emitter's emitted shape, with a docstring explaining the composition (the emitter orders; this fixture proves the ordered vector's transfer semantics in that exact shape). Taps: use env-bound tap host fn (works on all four routes per my trace-of machinery). Program sketch (raw nodes):\n\n```\n(app (lam ['r]                       ; with-k: first pass r = continuation\n      (if-node (= r :done)           ; after transfer, r = :done\n               (conj [] <log-target?>...)\n```\nHmm — I need the LOG to survive the escape. The log is HOST state (atom) — taps write to the atom regardless of VM control flow ✓. Program:\n\n```\n(app (v 'conj) (lit [])                                   ; run vector AFTER? no...\n```\nSimplest:\n```\n(with-k (if (= r 7)\n           [:done]                                 ; re-entry: delivered 7... \n           (assoc-app {} (tap :early) (tap :early-v) (r 7) (tap :never))))\n```\nwhere `r` in operand position = the continuation VALUE (variable) — `(app (v 'r) (lit 7))` invokes it → transfer → the pending assoc constructor (and its remaining operands incl. (tap :never)) is discarded → control resumes at the with-k capture point with r=7 → takes the `= 7` branch → value [:done]... wait — value of the program = the if's consequent. The log should contain [:early :early-v] and NOT :never. Expected value: the consequent — make it something distinctive like :transferred.\n\nOperand vector order in the fixture: I control the raw order — put the escape third of four operands: [seed {} (tap :early) (tap :early-v) (r 7) (tap :never)] — as literal-ish pairs — pair shape irrelevant here (raw). This asserts: operands consumed left-to-right; transfer at operand 5 discards the pending constructor; operand 6 never runs.\n\nAlso for the FAILURE (exception) half — strengthen the existing block: derive the expected prefix independently + assert every route raises (verify empirically what each route does first).\n\nLet me check what the current failure block looks like in the formatted file, then run an empirical probe of per-lane failure behavior + the raw transfer fixture on all four lanes via a scratch test before writing the final version.","signature":"ad435f3b1aaa4274a2e91f9f"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b22ab98-3808-492f-99b7-216dd4cf8345","uuid":"0b193063-4bec-40be-ae39-45b1885c74cd","timestamp":"2026-11-19T15:41:37.086Z","thinking_duration_ms":23377,"thinking_display":